{"goal": "The final value column must be numeric while preserving populated information. Decide the justified next action using the observed values. Do not invent meanings or discard information.", "column": "value", "observed_values": ["$100", "five hundred", "banana", "ERROR_404", null], "coverage": "Every value in the test input is shown.", "version": "V1", "request_guide": "Before every request, choose ONE justified next action from the current working_context. Check its complete argument layout below. Replace placeholders with actual observed columns, your own evidence-based explanation and actual candidate IDs. These are examples of structure, not instructions to perform these methods.

inspect_data: always include reason AND view. If view is column, patterns or values, also include column. Allowed views: schema, column, patterns, values, duplicates.
The values view accepts offset (default 0) and limit (1–100, default 50). Column inspection includes value_overview with full-column counts, common/rare format examples and explicit displayed-value coverage. To inspect an unseen page, use {"name":"inspect_data","arguments":{"reason":"Explain which unseen formats may change the repair","view":"values","column":"<column>","offset":50,"limit":50}}. Use the returned next_offset, not a guessed offset. Review rare values as well as the frequent ones; counts scanning every row does not mean every raw value was displayed.
{"name":"inspect_data","arguments":{"reason":"Explain what this inspection will clarify","view":"column","column":"<column>"}}

test_method for a builtin: always include hypothesis, columns, operation and parameters. Naming a method in the hypothesis does NOT select it: supply operation explicitly. Keep columns outside parameters. Use the tool schema for valid method options. Supply {} for a method with no options.
Before testing either kind of method, include value_assessment with four nonblank strings: evidence (version, observed coverage and formats), interpretation (justified repair rules), preservation (how populated values retain information), and ambiguities (specific unresolved meanings, or why none remain). The hypothesis and implementation must follow that assessment. No extra tool call is needed to provide this assessment.
{"name":"test_method","arguments":{"hypothesis":"Explain observed evidence, why this method fits, expected effect and assumptions","columns":["<column>"],"operation":"convert_data_types","parameters":{"target_type":"boolean","errors":"raise"}}}

test_method for a generated method: include hypothesis, columns, code and checks; omit operation and parameters. code must define the complete transform(df) function. checks must name selected columns and valid outcomes. Explain why a generated method is needed and what its sandbox checks establish.
Derive the implementation from inspected values and failure evidence: identify input patterns, choose justified output rules, implement those rules, and check their outcomes. Give a concise rationale in hypothesis. The example below illustrates the call layout only; choose your own algorithm for the actual defect. If a builtin cannot implement your rule, supply generated code rather than naming the proposed method in hypothesis. After failure, revise the actual transformation; changing only explanations or checks is not a repair. Inspect missing evidence or flag a specific unresolved business decision instead of inventing values.
{"name":"test_method","arguments":{"hypothesis":"Explain the observed whitespace defect and why the generated method is needed","columns":["<column>"],"code":"def transform(df):\n    df['<column>'] = df['<column>'].str.strip()\n    return df","checks":[{"column":"<column>","kind":"string"}]}}

Passing tests are applied automatically. Read applied, version and remaining. Do NOT call apply_candidate again after applied=true. Do not repeat a step marked done. Numeric conversion does not impute missing values; never claim it did.

apply_candidate is only for an existing passing candidate whose application has not succeeded. Include candidate_id and assessment of its actual measured trial results. Never invent an ID or assessment outcome.
{"name":"apply_candidate","arguments":{"candidate_id":"<returned candidate ID>","assessment":"Explain the measured trial results and why application is justified"}}

flag_for_review: include columns and reason when business meaning or a justified repair is unresolved.
{"name":"flag_for_review","arguments":{"columns":["<column>"],"reason":"Explain the specific unresolved decision"}}

finish: include summary and, if needed, unresolved. Describe only measured outcomes. Check current pending types and missing values before claiming completion.
The unresolved list replaces your earlier finish assessment; use [] when those concerns have been resolved. Earlier assessments remain in the audit history. Dataset checks, human-review flags and approvals are independent and cannot be cleared by this list.
{"name":"finish","arguments":{"summary":"Summarize actual applied changes and blockers","unresolved":[]}}

Final check: registered tool name; all fields for that action present; correct nesting; nonblank explanation; actual columns/IDs; parameters match the selected operation. If rejected, correct the named field before retrying. Emit a native structured tool call, or ONLY a complete JSON name/arguments object as shown; no surrounding prose or plan.
"}
